WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.951
A stranger receives a professional message. Instead of

00:00:03.951 --> 00:00:07.029
answering what it says, he becomes interested in

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:07.478
whether

00:00:07.478 --> 00:00:09.305
software touched its grammar.

00:00:14.175 --> 00:00:17.585
Someone may write in a second language, seek clarity

00:00:17.585 --> 00:00:17.782
and

00:00:17.782 --> 00:00:20.734
still be treated as if the improvement itself

00:00:20.734 --> 00:00:21.390
discredits

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:22.163
the speaker.

00:00:26.675 --> 00:00:30.071
Before mocking a junior colleague, imagine announcing

00:00:30.071 --> 00:00:30.199
to

00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:33.535
your boss or an established native speaker that their

00:00:33.535 --> 00:00:35.549
polished prose makes them a bot.

00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:43.950
For years academics were instructed, and sometimes

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.570
charged, to have manuscripts professionally edited.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:47.783
The

00:00:47.783 --> 00:00:51.545
expectation of standardized language did not originate

00:00:51.545 --> 00:00:52.103
with AI.

00:00:58.217 --> 00:01:02.218
Software assistance does not automatically certify a

00:01:02.218 --> 00:01:02.449
bad

00:01:02.449 --> 00:01:05.926
argument; nor does an unedited mistake certify

00:01:05.926 --> 00:01:06.833
originality.

00:01:11.592 --> 00:01:15.108
The essay challenges an etiquette where suspicion is

00:01:15.108 --> 00:01:18.482
directed disproportionately at people without the

00:01:18.482 --> 00:01:18.895
status

00:01:18.895 --> 00:01:19.910
to answer back.
